title: Storm-2949 / Entra ID SSPR
page: breaches/storm-2949-entra-sspr.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Microsoft assesses with high confidence that Storm-2949 got in through social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse; it then rode existing Azure roles to data theft.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: Dozens | Key Vault secrets taken | in roughly four minutes
stat: None | malware at initial access | identity abuse only
Thousands of files were downloaded in one action through the OneDrive web interface. Storm-2949 is Microsoft's designation for an activity cluster not yet attributed. Victim organizations were not named.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with. The page draws on the analysis Microsoft Threat Intelligence published on May 18, 2026.

Dozens of Key Vault secrets were read inside roughly four minutes. Key Vault is Azure's service for storing secrets, keys and certificates. Those secrets included database connection strings and identity credentials, which Microsoft notes dramatically expanded the attack's blast radius.

Thousands of files were downloaded in one action through the OneDrive web interface. The page calls that one instance, and Microsoft says the pattern was repeated across all the compromised user accounts.

There was no malware at initial access: identity abuse only.

Storm-2949 is Microsoft's designation for an activity cluster not yet attributed. Victim organizations were not named. Microsoft says the victims included IT personnel and senior leadership, and the objective was to exfiltrate as much data as possible from high-value assets.

Keep the words dozens, thousands and roughly when you say these aloud. The page gives no exact counts.

== What Microsoft reported on May 18, 2026
layout: text
Microsoft assesses with high confidence that Storm-2949 used social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse to get a target's MFA prompt approved.
- The actor then enrolled its own Authenticator device.
- Existing privileged Azure roles reached secrets, databases, storage and VMs.
notes:
Here is the page's summary in brief.

Microsoft Threat Intelligence published its analysis of Storm-2949 on May 18, 2026. Microsoft assesses with high confidence that the actor used a social engineering technique consistent with known abuses of Microsoft Entra ID self-service password reset, or SSPR. In that kind of abuse, an attacker starts a reset on a target's behalf and then persuades the target to approve the resulting multi-factor authentication (MFA) prompt.

Microsoft's example of the lure is an actor who might impersonate internal IT support, tell the user the account needs urgent verification, and ask them to approve the prompt as part of a routine password reset. Security Boulevard reports that Storm-2949 phoned its targets this way.

With the account in hand, the actor removed the victim's existing authentication methods and enrolled its own Microsoft Authenticator device. It then rode existing privileged Azure role-based access control (RBAC) assignments across multiple subscriptions into App Service publishing profiles, Key Vault secrets, SQL databases, storage accounts and virtual machines.

Victim organizations were not named. Microsoft says the victims included IT personnel and senior leadership.

== How sure Microsoft says it is
layout: compare
col: The way in | High confidence, per Microsoft | Social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse | Fake IT support: Microsoft's example | Phone call: per Security Boulevard
col: More of Microsoft's assessments | Account searches were likely meant to find privileged identities | Victims included IT personnel and senior leadership: deliberate targeting | From user roles and findings: likely an organized, convincing phishing scheme
The high-confidence assessment covers the technique, not the call.
notes:
The page is explicit about what Microsoft's confidence covers, and this slide keeps those words together.

Microsoft assesses with high confidence that Storm-2949 used a social engineering technique consistent with known abuses of the SSPR process. Microsoft explains that in such attacks an attacker starts the reset on behalf of a targeted user and then persuades that user to approve MFA prompts that appear legitimate. A fake IT support representative claiming urgent verification is Microsoft's example of that lure. The phone call comes from Security Boulevard, which reports that Storm-2949 called its targets this way. Keep each of those attributions when you retell stage 01.

The page reports more of Microsoft's readings. At stage 03, the actor searched Entra ID for accounts by name pattern and role attribute, which Microsoft assesses was likely intended to identify privileged identities and additional high-value targets. The victims included IT personnel and senior leadership, a selection Microsoft says indicated deliberate targeting. And based on the roles of the compromised users and the investigation findings, Microsoft assesses that the actor likely used an organized and convincing phishing scheme.

Keep the words likely and assesses with each of them.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | SSPR abuse | T1621
step: 02 | Persistence | Method takeover | T1556.006
step: 03 | Discovery | Graph queries | T1087.004
step: 04 | Privilege Escalation | Inherited roles | T1078.004
step: 05 | Credential Access | Key Vault reads | T1555
step: 06 | Lateral Movement | SQL and storage | T1528
step: 07 | Execution & Defense Impairment | Into the VMs | T1651
step: 08 | Exfiltration | Files and blobs | T1530
Arrows follow the page's order. Stage 01 is Microsoft's high-confidence assessment.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's eight stages, each with its phase and the first ATT&CK technique the page lists.

Stage 01 is initial access: social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse, which Microsoft assesses with high confidence. Stage 02, persistence, swapped the account's MFA methods for the attacker's own device. Stage 03, discovery, enumerated the tenant through Microsoft Graph. Stage 04, privilege escalation, used roles the accounts already held and the publishxml operation to reach App Service. Stage 05, credential access, read Key Vault secrets. Stage 06, lateral movement, opened Azure SQL and obtained storage keys. Stage 07, execution and defense impairment, reached into virtual machines and went after Defender. Stage 08, exfiltration, took files and blobs.

The arrows follow the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== High confidence, per Microsoft: social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1621 | MFA Request Generation
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Mechanism | an attacker-started reset, then the user persuaded to approve
fact: Microsoft's example | might impersonate IT support, claiming urgent verification
fact: Phone call | Security Boulevard reports Storm-2949 phoned its targets
fact: Victims included | IT personnel and senior leadership
The MFA prompt was genuine, so number matching could not tell the difference.
notes:
The page is explicit about what Microsoft's confidence covers here. Microsoft assesses with high confidence that Storm-2949 used a social engineering technique consistent with known abuses of the self-service password reset process. In such attacks, Microsoft explains, an attacker starts the SSPR flow on behalf of a targeted user, then persuades that user to approve MFA prompts that appear legitimate.

Microsoft's example of the lure: the attacker might impersonate an internal IT support representative and contact the user, claiming the account needs urgent verification and instructing them to approve the prompts as part of a routine password reset. Present that as Microsoft's example. Security Boulevard reports that Storm-2949 called its targets on the phone this way, and the page's phase label calls it vishing, meaning voice phishing.

The page maps the stage to T1621, Multi-Factor Authentication Request Generation, and T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

The prompt was genuine, so number matching and push approval bought nothing. The page does not describe what the target saw on the prompt. The victims included IT personnel and senior leadership, users likely to hold privileged roles, a selection Microsoft says indicated deliberate targeting.

The page names the gap: SSPR accepted push and number-match approval instead of phishing-resistant verification. The next slide explains SSPR and number matching in general terms.

== What SSPR and number matching are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, How it works: Microsoft Entra self-service password reset | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/concept-sspr-howitworks
credit: Also from How number matching works in MFA push notifications for Authenticator (learn.microsoft.com)
SSPR lets users reset their own password without an administrator or the help desk, using the authentication methods an admin policy requires.

With Authenticator, SSPR requires number matching: the user enters a number shown on the sign-in page.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two ideas in general, from Microsoft Learn, and says nothing about the incident.

Microsoft Entra self-service password reset gives users the ability to change or reset their password with no administrator or help desk involvement. A user who starts a reset enters a user ID and passes a captcha. Microsoft Entra ID then checks that the user is enabled for SSPR and has the authentication methods the administrator policy requires, one or two depending on the policy. If the checks pass, the user is guided through resetting the password.

Number matching is enabled for all Microsoft Authenticator push notifications, and SSPR is one of the scenarios it covers. During SSPR, the sign-in page shows a number that the user needs to enter into the Authenticator notification.

Also from How number matching works in MFA push notifications for Authenticator: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/how-to-mfa-number-match

== Existing methods stripped, an attacker Authenticator device enrolled
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Persistence
technique: T1556.006 | Modify Authentication Process: MFA
technique: T1098.005 | Account Manipulation: Device Registration
fact: Removed | phone numbers, email addresses and Authenticator registrations
fact: Effect | MFA and Conditional Access now attest the attacker's device
fact: Real owner | lost the methods needed to recover the account
fact: Tried and failed | credentials on a service principal, for lack of permissions
That single change turned MFA from a control into an asset.
notes:
Holding the reset flow, the actor rewrote the account's authentication posture rather than simply using it. Registered phone numbers, email addresses and prior Microsoft Authenticator registrations were removed, and an attacker-controlled Authenticator device was enrolled in their place. The page maps this to T1556.006, Modify Authentication Process: Multi-Factor Authentication, and T1098.005, Account Manipulation: Device Registration.

The page says that single change turned MFA from a control into an asset. Every later sign-in satisfied MFA and Conditional Access policy using the attacker's device, while the real owner lost the exact methods they would have needed to recover the account. Conditional Access is the Entra ID feature that sets the conditions a sign-in must meet.

The actor also tried to add credentials to a compromised Entra service principal, an identity an application uses. Those credentials would have survived any user-side password reset. The attempt failed for lack of permissions.

The detection signal the page names is a Microsoft Defender XDR alert for suspicious addition of a default third-party MFA method to a user account.

== Custom Python scripts enumerated the tenant through Microsoft Graph
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Discovery
technique: T1087.004 | Account Discovery: Cloud Account
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
fact: Interface | Microsoft Graph API, driven by custom Python scripts
fact: Enumerated | users by name pattern and role attribute, groups, applications, service principals
fact: Purpose, per Microsoft | likely to identify privileged identities and more high-value targets
Valid-session Graph reads look ordinary unless volume and pattern are baselined.
notes:
Instead of clicking through the portal, the actor issued automated requests to the Microsoft Graph API using custom Python scripts, enumerating users and applications within the tenant. A tenant is one organization's own instance of Entra ID. The page maps the stage to T1087.004, Account Discovery: Cloud Account, and T1580, Cloud Infrastructure Discovery.

It searched Entra ID for accounts by name pattern and role attribute, which Microsoft assesses was likely intended to identify privileged identities and additional high-value targets. It also mapped the applications and service principals registered in the tenant. The stage's details list groups among what was enumerated.

Keep the words assesses and likely: the purpose is Microsoft's reading.

The page says Graph reads of this kind are authenticated, expected and high volume in most tenants. Scripted enumeration from a legitimate compromised account produces very little that stands out without behavior baselining on request volume and pattern.

The next slide explains Microsoft Graph in general terms.

== What Microsoft Graph is
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Overview of Microsoft Graph | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview
Microsoft Graph is one API, at graph.microsoft.com, for data in Microsoft cloud services including Microsoft Entra and Microsoft 365.

Code reaches it through REST requests or SDKs. Only authorized callers can access the data.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft describes Graph as the gateway to data and intelligence in Microsoft cloud services like Microsoft Entra and Microsoft 365. The Microsoft Graph API offers a single endpoint, https://graph.microsoft.com, and applications reach it through REST APIs or SDKs. An API is a programming interface, and an SDK is a software development kit.

Graph follows relationships between objects. Microsoft's own example looks up a user in Microsoft Entra ID, then the user's manager, then the groups the user is a member of, then the other members of each group. Among the common requests Microsoft lists are the users and the groups in an organization.

Microsoft Graph is secured, and only authorized callers can access the data.

== Roles the accounts already held opened App Service and Kudu
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Privilege Escalation
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
fact: Already held | privileged custom Azure RBAC roles on several subscriptions
fact: ARM operation | publishxml, which retrieves the App Service publishing profile
fact: Inside the profile | FTP and Web Deploy deployment credentials
fact: Then reached | the Kudu console: files, environment variables, commands
A permission that reads like a deployment convenience delivered command execution.
notes:
The page says no exploit was required at this stage. The compromised identities already carried privileged custom Azure RBAC roles on several subscriptions, so the actor simply used the authorization the accounts were already holding. The page maps the stage to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts, and T1552, Unsecured Credentials.

It called microsoft.Web/sites/publishxml/action to retrieve App Service publishing profiles. App Service is Azure's service for hosting web apps. Each profile carries FTP and Web Deploy deployment credentials inside it, and the actor used those to reach the Kudu console. The page explains Kudu as the built-in administrative interface for Azure App Services, which lets an authenticated user browse the file system, inspect environment variables and execute commands in the application's own context.

So an Azure Resource Manager (ARM) permission that reads like a deployment convenience delivered interactive command execution inside production web applications. The page puts the gap this way: publishxml is classed as a management action, but it functions as credential issuance.

The next slide explains App Service deployment credentials in general terms.

== What App Service deployment credentials are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Manage deployment credentials for Azure App Service | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-configure-credentials
credit: Also from Disable basic authentication in Azure App Service deployments (learn.microsoft.com)
Deployment credentials are separate from Azure subscription credentials. App-scope credentials are one set per app, created with it.

FTP and Web Deploy clients connect with a username and password, which Microsoft calls basic authentication.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Deployment credentials let you deploy to Azure App Service by local Git or FTP/S, and they are different from your Azure subscription credentials. There are two kinds. User-scope credentials are one set for a user's entire Azure account. App-scope credentials are one set per app, generated automatically when the app is created, and usable to deploy that app only. Azure CLI retrieves the app-scope credentials with the command az webapp deployment list-publishing-profiles.

App Service provides basic authentication for FTP and Web Deploy clients to connect using username and password deployment credentials. Microsoft contrasts this with Microsoft Entra access tokens, which have a limited usable lifetime and are specific to the applications and resources they are issued for. When basic authentication is disabled, deployment methods that depend on it no longer work, and an FTP connection gets a 401 Unauthenticated message.

Also from Disable basic authentication in Azure App Service deployments: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/configure-basic-auth-disable

== Dozens of secrets in roughly four minutes
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1555 | Credentials from Password Stores
fact: First | access configuration modified, a control-plane operation
fact: Then | dozens of Key Vault secrets read, a data-plane operation
fact: Secrets included | database connection strings and identity credentials
fact: Permission model | not named on the page
An actor with sufficient control-plane rights can grant itself data-plane access and spend it long before a human reviews the policy change.
notes:
The actor manipulated Key Vault access configurations, then read dozens of secrets out of the vault, all inside roughly four minutes. The page maps this to T1555, Credentials from Password Stores.

Those secrets included database connection strings and identity credentials, which Microsoft notes dramatically expanded the attack's blast radius. Blast radius means how much an attacker can reach from one foothold.

The page draws the structural lesson from the order of events. Changing who may read a vault is an Azure Resource Manager control-plane operation, while reading the secrets is a data-plane operation. The control plane manages a resource; the data plane is the content inside it. An actor with sufficient control-plane rights can grant itself data-plane access and spend it long before a human reviews the policy change.

The page names the gap: no alert on the access-configuration change, and no rate anomaly raised on bulk secret reads. It does not say which permission model the vaults used, and the slide says so.

The next slide explains Key Vault's two permission models in general terms.

== Key Vault's two permission models
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Azure RBAC vs. access policies (legacy) | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/rbac-access-policy
credit: Also from Secure your Azure Key Vault (learn.microsoft.com)
Key Vault has a control plane, for managing the vault, and a data plane, for its keys, secrets and certificates.

Under legacy access policies, Microsoft warns, users with Contributor or Key Vault Contributor can grant themselves data plane access.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Azure Key Vault protects cryptographic keys, certificates and secrets such as connection strings and passwords. Access is controlled through two interfaces: the control plane, for managing Key Vault itself, and the data plane, for working with keys, secrets and certificates.

Key Vault offers two authorization systems. Azure RBAC operates on both the control and data planes. It grants access through role assignments, each made of a security principal, a role definition and a scope, and the right to grant access to keys, secrets and certificates requires the Owner or User Access Administrator role. The legacy access policy model is native to Key Vault and operates on the data plane alone.

Microsoft warns that under the access policy model, users with the Contributor or Key Vault Contributor role, or any role that includes Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/write permissions, can grant themselves data plane access by configuring an access policy. Microsoft recommends the RBAC permission model instead.

Also from Secure your Azure Key Vault: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/secure-key-vault

== firewallrules/write opened Azure SQL, and listkeys produced storage keys
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Azure SQL | attacker addresses added to the server firewall, then sign-in
fact: Credentials used | database connection strings taken from Key Vault at stage 05
fact: Storage | network access changed; listkeys gave account keys, SAS tokens
Not one of these operations reads a byte of customer data by itself. Chained, they are a complete path from ARM rights to live production data.
notes:
The page says the entire intrusion turned on this step. Holding connection strings from Key Vault, the actor still needed a network path. It called microsoft.sql/servers/firewallrules/write to add its own addresses to the Azure SQL server firewall, then signed in to the databases with the harvested credentials.

In parallel it used microsoft.storage/storageaccounts/write to manipulate storage account network access configuration, and microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/listkeys/action to obtain account keys and shared access signature (SAS) tokens. The page maps the stage to T1528, Steal Application Access Token.

Not one of those operations reads a byte of customer data by itself, and every one of them looks like ordinary infrastructure management on a change ticket. Chained, the page says, they are a complete path from Azure Resource Manager rights to live production data.

The next slide explains SAS tokens and Azure SQL firewall rules in general terms.

== What SAS tokens and Azure SQL firewall rules are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Grant limited access with shared access signatures | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-sas-overview
credit: Also from Azure SQL Database IP firewall rules (learn.microsoft.com)
A SAS is a token appended to a storage resource's URI and signed with a key. Service and account SAS are signed with the storage account key.

Azure SQL's server-level firewall blocks connections from addresses no rule allows.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

A shared access signature (SAS) provides delegated access to resources in a storage account, with control over which resources a client may access, with what permissions, and for how long. It is a token appended to the resource's URI, and its signature is signed with the key that was used to create the SAS. A service SAS and an account SAS are both signed with the storage account key. Microsoft notes that the generation of SAS tokens cannot be audited, and that Azure Storage does not track SAS tokens in any way.

When you create a logical server in Azure SQL Database, a server-level firewall blocks all access to its public endpoint. Connection attempts must pass through the firewall before they reach the server or a database. Server-level IP firewall rules let clients reach the whole server, meaning every database it manages. A connection from an address outside every database-level and server-level rule fails.

Also from Azure SQL Database IP firewall rules: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/firewall-configure

== From the control plane into VMs: a backdoor admin, scripts, Defender tampering
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Execution & Defense Impairment
technique: T1651 | Cloud Administration Command
technique: T1685 | Disable or Modify Tools
fact: VMAccess extension | a backdoor local administrator account on a targeted VM
fact: Run Command | scripts, including a failed attempt on a managed identity
fact: Defender | attempts to disable real-time protection, behavior monitoring
fact: ScreenConnect | installed; later used to exfiltrate .pfx files from some hosts
From the control plane into the guest OS, then it set about blinding the host.
notes:
From the Azure control plane the actor reached into the guest operating system. It deployed the VMAccess extension to create a backdoor local administrator account on a targeted virtual machine (VM), and used Run Command to execute scripts. The page maps this to T1651, Cloud Administration Command.

One script tried to abuse a VM managed identity, requesting an access token from the Azure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) to retrieve secrets from the production web app's Key Vault. That attempt failed because the managed identity lacked the permissions needed.

A PowerShell script attempted to disable Microsoft Defender Antivirus real-time protection and behavior monitoring, and to interfere with the associated service; the page tags this T1685, Disable or Modify Tools. The script installed ScreenConnect, a remote monitoring and management tool, from attacker infrastructure, and attempted to rename or configure the service to resemble a Windows component. It also attempted cleanup: clearing Windows event logs and deleting command history and temporary files, which the page links to T1685.005, Clear Windows Event Logs.

The actor then used ScreenConnect to run commands across multiple compromised devices. On a subset of those hosts it discovered and exfiltrated .pfx certificate files, which the page says may contain private keys and could be valuable for follow-on access.

The next slide explains Run Command and VM managed identities.

== What Run Command and a VM's managed identity do
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Run scripts in your VM by using Run Command | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/run-command-overview
credit: Also from How to use managed identities on an Azure VM to acquire an access token (learn.microsoft.com)
Run Command runs scripts inside an Azure VM through the VM agent.

Code on a VM requests access tokens for the VM's managed identity from the Instance Metadata Service. Microsoft says all code on the VM can request them.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Run Command uses the virtual machine agent to run scripts within an Azure Windows or Linux VM. Microsoft describes it as a tool for machine or application management, for example to diagnose and fix VM access and network issues. The original action commands run as the System account or root.

Managed identities give Azure services an automatically managed identity in Microsoft Entra ID, which can authenticate to any service that supports Entra authentication without credentials in code. On a VM, a client requests an access token for that identity from the Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) endpoint, at 169.254.169.254, sending a Metadata header that Microsoft describes as a mitigation against server side request forgery. The request names the resource the token is for.

Microsoft states that the security boundary of a managed identity is the resource where it is used: all code and scripts running on a VM can request and retrieve tokens for any managed identity available on it.

Also from How to use managed identities for Azure resources on an Azure VM to acquire an access token: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/how-to-use-vm-token

== Thousands of files in one action, and blobs drained over multiple days
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1530 | Data from Cloud Storage
technique: T1213.002 | Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint
fact: Targeted | OneDrive and SharePoint, for VPN and remote access documents
fact: One instance | thousands of files in one action, OneDrive web interface
fact: Per Microsoft | the pattern repeated across all compromised accounts
fact: Azure Storage | blobs, by a custom Python script, over multiple days
VPN and remote access documents make the next intrusion cheaper, the page says.
notes:
Exfiltration ran on two tracks. In Microsoft 365 the actor targeted OneDrive and SharePoint, concentrating on IT documents covering virtual private network (VPN) configuration and remote access procedures, which the page calls precisely the material that makes the next intrusion cheaper. In one instance it used the OneDrive web interface to download thousands of files in a single action to its own infrastructure, a pattern Microsoft says was repeated across all the compromised user accounts. The page maps the stage to T1530, Data from Cloud Storage, and T1213.002, Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint.

In Azure it ran a custom Python script built on the Azure SDK for Storage to enumerate and download blobs directly to an attacker-controlled endpoint device. Blobs are objects held in Azure Blob Storage. That storage exfiltration continued over multiple days from the initial access, with the actor alternating between secret-based and OAuth-based authentication as access conditions and controls shifted around it. The page does not say which secrets or tokens it used.

== Four ARM operations the page calls paths to production data
layout: table
row: Operation | Stage | What it did here
row: publishxml | 04 | FTP and Web Deploy credentials, then the Kudu console
row: firewallrules/write | 06 | attacker addresses added to the Azure SQL server firewall
row: storageaccounts/write | 06 | storage account network access configuration changed
row: listkeys | 06 | storage account keys and SAS tokens
Each reads as management, the page says, and each is, in practice, a direct path to production data.
notes:
The page draws one pattern through stages 04 to 06: management permissions that behave as data access.

Each row is an Azure Resource Manager (ARM) operation the page names. ARM is the layer that handles Azure's management requests. At stage 04, publishxml, which the page says is classed as a management action, functioned as credential issuance. At stage 06, firewallrules/write opened the Azure SQL server firewall to attacker addresses, storageaccounts/write changed storage network access configuration, and listkeys produced account keys and SAS tokens. The page says listkeys and firewallrules/write are classed as management permissions but behave as direct data access.

Stage 05 shows the same shape inside Key Vault. Changing who may read a vault is a control-plane operation, and reading the secrets is a data-plane one.

In the page's defense, every one of these four reads as management, and every one is, in practice, a direct path to production data.

== Six defenses against eight stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Phishing-resistant reset | 01
row: Alert on method changes | 02
row: Gate registration, basic auth off | 02 | 04
row: Monitor ARM operations, just-in-time roles | 04 | 06 | 07
row: Key Vault on four axes | 05
row: Tamper protection and published indicators | 07
No defense on the page names stage 03's Graph enumeration or stage 08's downloads.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its eight stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's instruction names something only that stage has.

Phishing-resistant reset names the prompt Storm-2949 generated at stage 01. Alerting on method changes names stage 02's method removals, new device enrollment and service principal attempt. Gating registration names re-enrolling MFA, stage 02, and turning off basic authentication names the FTP and Web Deploy path of stage 04's publishing profiles. Monitoring ARM operations names stage 04's privileged custom roles and publishxml, stage 06's listkeys, firewallrules/write and storageaccounts/write, and stage 07's VMAccess, Run Command and IMDS token. Key Vault hardening names stage 05's access-configuration change and dozens of reads inside four minutes. Tamper protection names stage 07's PowerShell attempts, and one published indicator is the attacker's ScreenConnect instance from stage 07. The other two indicators are egress addresses the page ties to no stage.

Columns 03 and 08 are empty. No defense's instruction names stage 03's Graph enumeration, or stage 08's OneDrive and SharePoint downloads or blobs. Microsoft's CloudStorageAggregatedEvents table, for storage data-plane access, comes near stage 08, but the page does not tie it there. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Protect the reset, and alert when methods change
layout: compare
col: Phishing-resistant reset (stage 01) | SSPR mints a working credential for anyone who can satisfy it | Require FIDO2 keys, passkeys or Windows Hello for Business | For reset and every privileged account
col: Method changes (stage 02) | Removals, then a new enrollment, show in the audit log | Treat all methods removed, then one new Authenticator, as an incident | Watch for credentials added to service principals
Number matching alone does not stop this: the prompt Storm-2949 generated was real.
notes:
Two defenses answer stages 01 and 02.

The page treats self-service password reset as a credential-issuance system: it mints a working credential for anyone who can satisfy it, so it deserves the same scrutiny as an admin role grant. Require phishing-resistant methods in the Entra ID Authentication Methods policy for reset and for every privileged account: FIDO2 security keys, passkeys, or Windows Hello for Business. FIDO2 is a standard for sign-in with a security key or passkey. Number matching alone does not stop this. The prompt Storm-2949 generated was real, and a user who has just been told by IT to expect it will approve it.

Second, alert on authentication method registration changes, not only on sign-ins. The page says the whole takeover is visible in the Entra ID audit log as method removals followed by a new device enrollment. Treat all existing methods removed, then one new Authenticator registered, as an incident rather than a help-desk event. Enable the Defender XDR detection for suspicious addition of a default third-party MFA method to a user account.

Monitor for credentials being added to service principals as well: Storm-2949 tried exactly that and was stopped only by a permissions gap.

== Gate MFA registration, and turn off basic authentication
layout: text
Apply Conditional Access to the Register security information user action.
- Require a compliant or hybrid-joined device, or a trusted network.
- Disable legacy authentication tenant-wide.
- Turn off basic authentication on App Services, removing stage 04's FTP and Web Deploy path.
notes:
This defense answers stages 02 and 04.

Gate the registration flow itself with Conditional Access. Apply a policy to the Register security information user action that requires a compliant or hybrid-joined device, or a trusted network. The page says a caller working from an unmanaged device then cannot re-enroll MFA at all, the step stage 02 relied on.

In the same pass, disable legacy authentication tenant-wide and turn off basic authentication on App Services. The page says that removes the FTP and Web Deploy path that the stolen publishing profiles depended on at stage 04.

== Treat ARM management permissions as paths to data
layout: compare
col: Roles (04) | Audit Azure RBAC role assignments regularly | Replace standing privileged custom roles with just-in-time elevation
col: Operations (04, 06) | Alert in activity logs on publishxml and listkeys | And on firewallrules/write and storageaccounts/write
col: VMs (07) | Restrict VMAccess extension deployment and Run Command | Scope each VM managed identity to least privilege
The page says each of the four alerted operations is, in practice, a direct path to production data.
notes:
This defense answers stages 04, 06 and 07. The page's instruction is to stop treating Azure Resource Manager management permissions as harmless, and to monitor the specific operations.

For stage 04, audit Azure RBAC role assignments regularly, and replace standing privileged custom roles with just-in-time elevation, meaning a role granted only for a limited time when needed.

Then monitor the specific operations. Alert in Azure Monitor activity logs on microsoft.Web/sites/publishxml/action, microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/listkeys/action, microsoft.sql/servers/firewallrules/write and microsoft.storage/storageaccounts/write. The page says every one of those reads as management and every one is, in practice, a direct path to production data.

For stage 07, restrict VMAccess extension deployment and Run Command the same way. Scope each VM managed identity to least privilege, so that a stolen IMDS token is worth as little as possible.

== Harden Key Vault on all four axes
layout: table
row: Axis | What the page asks
row: RBAC | Azure RBAC authorization rather than legacy access policies
row: Network | the vault behind its firewall and private endpoints
row: Purge protection | soft delete and purge protection enabled
row: Logging | diagnostic logs streamed to your SIEM
Alert on any access-configuration or role-assignment change on a vault, and on bulk secret reads: dozens inside four minutes should page someone.
notes:
This defense answers stage 05, where Key Vault access configuration was changed and dozens of secrets read inside roughly four minutes.

The page asks for hardening on four axes. Use Azure RBAC authorization rather than legacy access policies. Put the vault behind its firewall and private endpoints. Enable soft delete and purge protection. Stream diagnostic logs to your SIEM, the security information and event management system where logs are collected and alerted on.

Then alert on any access-configuration or role-assignment change on a vault, and on bulk secret-read volume. In the page's words, dozens of reads inside four minutes should page someone.

Remember the gap the page named at stage 05: no alert on the access-configuration change, and no rate anomaly raised on bulk secret reads.

== Turn on tamper protection, and hunt with the tables Microsoft named
layout: compare
col: Tamper protection (stage 07) | Blocks the exact PowerShell attempts this actor used | Against disabling real-time protection and behavior monitoring
col: Microsoft's hunting tables | CloudAuditEvents: Azure control-plane activity | CloudStorageAggregatedEvents: storage data-plane access
Microsoft lists 176.123.4[.]44 and 91.208.197[.]87 as attacker egress addresses, and 185.241.208[.]243 as the attacker's ScreenConnect instance.
notes:
The last defense answers stage 07 and gives the page's hunting material.

Turn on Defender tamper protection. The page says it blocks the exact PowerShell attempts to disable real-time protection and behavior monitoring that this actor used.

For retrospective hunting, Microsoft points at two Defender XDR advanced hunting tables: CloudAuditEvents for Azure control-plane activity, and CloudStorageAggregatedEvents for storage data-plane access.

The published indicators to sweep for are three addresses. Microsoft lists 176.123.4[.]44 and 91.208.197[.]87 as addresses the attacker egressed from, and 185.241.208[.]243 as the attacker's ScreenConnect instance, the tool installed at stage 07. The bracketed dot is how the page writes them, so they do not work as links.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Does SSPR require phishing-resistant methods, for reset and every privileged account?
check: Would all methods removed, then one new Authenticator, open an incident?
check: Can an unmanaged device register security information in your tenant?
check: Is basic authentication off on your App Services?
check: Do activity log alerts cover publishxml, listkeys, firewallrules/write and storageaccounts/write?
check: Would a vault access change, or dozens of reads in four minutes, page someone?
check: Is Defender tamper protection turned on?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether SSPR and every privileged account require phishing-resistant methods, and whether all existing methods removed, then one new Authenticator registered, would be treated as an incident rather than a help-desk event. Ask whether anyone on an unmanaged device can register security information.

Ask whether basic authentication is off on App Services, and whether activity log alerts cover publishxml, listkeys, firewallrules/write and storageaccounts/write. The defense behind that alert also asks you to replace standing privileged custom roles with just-in-time elevation, and to restrict VMAccess and Run Command.

Ask whether a Key Vault access change, or dozens of secret reads inside four minutes, would page someone. Finally, ask whether Defender tamper protection is on.

== Why did number matching not help at stage 01?
layout: question
The page says number matching and push approval bought nothing here.

What does number matching check, and what did it miss?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

At stage 01, Microsoft assesses with high confidence that Storm-2949 used social engineering consistent with known SSPR abuse: an attacker starts a reset for a chosen user and persuades them to approve the MFA prompt. Microsoft's example of the lure is someone impersonating IT support, and Security Boulevard reports that Storm-2949 phoned its targets this way. The page says number matching and push approval bought nothing.

Ask each person what number matching is designed to stop, and what it cannot know, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== It checked a real request, not who asked for it
layout: answer
The prompt was genuine: the control validated a real Entra ID request, not the person who asked for it.
- A user primed to expect it will approve it.
- The page's fix: phishing-resistant methods for reset and every privileged account.
notes:
The page's answer is that the prompt was genuine. The control was validating a real Entra ID request, not the identity of the person who asked for it. A user primed to expect the prompt will approve it, and number matching cannot tell the difference.

The page's fix follows from that. Require phishing-resistant methods, such as FIDO2 security keys, passkeys or Windows Hello for Business, in the Entra ID Authentication Methods policy for reset and for every privileged account.

Bring the group's answers back to this. The page names the gap at stage 01: SSPR accepted push and number-match approval instead of phishing-resistant verification.

== What would you add for stages 03 and 08?
layout: question
No defense on the page names stage 03's Graph enumeration, or stage 08's OneDrive, SharePoint and blob downloads.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty columns in the defense matrix.

At stage 03, custom Python scripts drove the Microsoft Graph API to enumerate users by name pattern and role attribute, groups, applications and service principals.

At stage 08, the actor targeted OneDrive and SharePoint, concentrating on VPN configuration and remote access documents. In one instance it downloaded thousands of files in a single action through the OneDrive web interface, a pattern Microsoft says was repeated across all the compromised user accounts. A custom Python script on the Azure SDK for Storage downloaded blobs over multiple days.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== No page defense names stage 03's enumeration or stage 08's downloads
layout: answer
Your proposals are your own. Two points on the page come near:
- Stage 03: scripted enumeration from a legitimate compromised account shows little without behavior baselining.
- Microsoft names CloudStorageAggregatedEvents for hunting storage data-plane access.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. No defense on the page names stage 03's Graph enumeration or stage 08's downloads, and this deck adds none of its own.

Two points come near. Stage 03 itself says scripted enumeration from a legitimate compromised account produces very little that stands out without behavior baselining on request volume and pattern. That is the page describing the stage, not one of its defenses. And in the last defense, Microsoft points at the CloudStorageAggregatedEvents table for storage data-plane access; the page does not tie it to stage 08. The group may argue that these reach stages 03 and 08; that argument is the group's own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports: Graph reads by name pattern and role attribute, thousands of files in a single action through the OneDrive web interface, and blobs downloaded over multiple days.

